I'm new to INLA so I was hoping I could post a follow up to better understand inla.posterior.sample. I have a model with fixed effects, temporal autocorrelation (AR1 model), and spatial autocorrelation (Besag model). I have 2900 "observations" in my data (some are NAs for predictive purposes). The latent posterior samples for my model have 2900 "Predictor.xxxx" samples, 2900 "ID.xxxx" samples, and 2900 "year.xxxx" samples, where ID and year are the replicates for the AR1 and Besag models, respectively, plus the coefficients for the intercept and covariate for the fixed effects.

Are the "Predictor.xxxx" samples the "fitted values" from the joint posterior and the "ID.xxxx" and "year.xxxx" the estimated error for their respective models?

Adam

On Monday, August 11, 2014 11:42:59 AM UTC-6, help help wrote:
> On Mon, 2014-08-11 at 07:58 -0700, Maëlle Salmon wrote:
> > Dear Help,
> >
> >  I am using INLA to fit a model of the negative binomial family with
> > log-link to some time series. My goal is to obtain a quantile of the
> > predictive posterior distribution of the response variable y_t.
> >
> > My current code works but I wonder if I could make it faster.
> >
> > Right now, after I have fit the model using INLA, I compute the
> > quantile through Monte Carlo sampling. I draw random samples
> > (inla.rmarginal) from the marginal for the mean of the negative
> > binomial distribution (marginals.fitted.values) and for the
> > overdispersion parameter theta (internal.marginals.hyperpar[[1]]). I
> > draw mc.mutheta values of the mean and of the parameter theta. This
> > gives me mc.mutheta (mean,theta)_j. For each of them I draw mc.y
> > values from the negative binomial distribution parameterized by
> > (mean,theta)_j. Thus I get a vector of mc.mutheta*mc.y values for the
> > response, upon which I apply the quantile() function to get the
> > quantile of the posterior predictive distribution I want. This is
> > quite compute-intensive.
> >
> > Therefore my question use: is there a faster way in INLA to get the
> > quantile of the predictive posterior distribution of the response
> > variable or to sample from this distribution?
>
>
> You're almost there, as you do not account for the dependence of the
> overdispersion parameter and the fitted.values, but I guess its almost
> right.
>
> if you want to sample from the joint, then look at
>
> inla.posterior.sample
>
> which needs a result-object r=
> inla(...,control.compute=list(config=TRUE)))
>
> just make sure that the fitted.values are computed with the correct link
> function (see the FAQ entry on the r-inla.org <http://r-inla.org> page).
>
>
> Best
> H
>
>
> --
> Håvard Rue
> he...@r-inla.org
